WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Brownfield integration requires a native Swift runtime and a JavaScript runtime to co-exist inside a single iOS process.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
They run side-by-side, executing logic concurrently.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.000
You cannot use Expo's managed workflow for this.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.000
A Brownfield host application already owns its Xcode project, its custom dependency graph, and its app delegate.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Expo cannot be layered on top of an existing native foundation.

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Instead, you must use the React Native CLI to manually weave the JavaScript workspace into your existing native iOS project.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Success in this environment relies entirely on two technical requirements, configuring static linkage at the build level, and fully utilizing React Native's new architecture.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Historically, integrations relied on the asynchronous bridge to pass messages between Swift and JavaScript.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
This created severe JSON serialization bottlenecks, frequently stalling the main thread during heavy data loads.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
The new architecture replaces that legacy bridge with the fabric renderer and JSI.

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:10.000
JSI allows C++ to act as a shared language between the environments, establishing zero serialization, synchronous communication.

12
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:18.000
The central challenge of successful integration lies in the rigorous management of the C++ boundary between these two active runtimes.

13
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
The physical integration between Swift and React Native starts at the package manager.

14
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Coco Pods routes all the required iOS dependencies.

15
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Explicitly set the fabric renderer flag to true in your pod file, routing compilation away from the deprecated paper renderer.

16
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Enabling the Hermes engine is also mandatory. It is the only officially supported JavaScript engine for the new architecture and must compile in tandem with fabric.

17
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Fabric introduces a strict build constraint. It requires static linkage across your entire dependency graph.

18
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:59.000
If you allow dynamic frameworks into this compilation step, the compiler will inevitably generate duplicate symbol errors at link time.

19
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:12.000
The build will fail unless static linkage is enforced. For the Pods resolution script to locate the required modules, place your package.json at the repository route, exactly one level above the iOS folder.

20
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:22.000
After resolving the pod file, always open the resulting.xc workspace file. The standard.xc project file will no longer compile the new dependencies.

21
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:29.000
This rigid Coco Pods dependency graph is the physical prerequisite that allows the C++ JSI layer to function.

22
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Moving from the build step to runtime memory, the anchor of the JavaScript engine is a class called RCT host.

23
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:45.000
An iOS process must instantiate exactly one RCT host. The single host initializes Hermes, manages the bundle, and owns the surface presenter.

24
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Violating the singleton rule by creating multiple hosts, wastes, memory limits, and triggers undefined behavior in JSI.

25
00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The host also controls how the JavaScript bundle is resolved. In development, it connects to the Metro server over port 8081.

26
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
In production, it reads an offline embedded bundle directly from the device.

27
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Never deallocate the RCT host unless all UI surfaces are confirmed unmounted and there is no asynchronous JavaScript work pending.

28
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Isolating the JavaScript engine inside a strict singleton prevents the secondary runtime from overwhelming the host device's memory.

29
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:30.000
The legacy RCT root B class used to mount react native views is completely deprecated in react native 0.76.

30
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:39.000
The modern entry point is RCT fabric surface. It operates as a lightweight view controller that receives its rendering coordination directly from the host.

31
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Swift passes initial load data to the surface using the initial properties dictionary.

32
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Because this relies on JSI, initial properties bypasses async cues entirely.

33
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:56.000
The data serializes synchronously across the boundary before the surface renders its first physical frame.

34
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.000
These initial properties are strictly read only at mount time.

35
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:12.000
If your application uses SwiftUI, wrap the native view controller inside a view UI controller representable struct, utilizing the make UI controller method for the initialization.

36
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:20.000
To prevent the surface from overlapping the native navigation bar, apply the .ignores safe area modifier on the SwiftUI side.

37
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.000
This explicitly hands control of the insets to react native safe area context.

38
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:37.000
By treating RCT fabric surface as a disposable lightweight child of the RCT host, engineers can embed cross-platform screens without disrupting the native SwiftUI architecture.

39
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:46.000
After a surface mounts, you require ongoing communication between Swift and JavaScript to trigger native behaviors like the iOS keychain or health kit.

40
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.000
Turbo modules are the required path for executing these native behaviors under the new architecture.

41
00:04:51.000 --> 00:05:01.000
This interoperability relies on Cogen. At build time, a TypeScript specification compiles into C++ glue code, physically linking the environments.

42
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:08.000
This C++ layer leverages JSI to cross into Swift synchronously, eliminating asynchronous round trips entirely.

43
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:16.000
When routing iOS universal links, if the UI surface isn't created yet, Swift must inject the URL directly into initial properties.

44
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:23.000
But if the react native surface is already mounted, Swift must push the URL through a turbo module eventometer for immediate delivery.

45
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Turbo modules bridge the runtime boundary, converting the slow asynchronous choke point into a synchronized C++ highway.

46
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:42.000
This is the complete brownfield stack. A static pod file foundation supports the RCT host pillar, which branches out to multiple fabric surface UI layers, all interconnected by synchronous C++ data lines.

47
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.000
After integration, a common runtime bug is a prolonged black screen appearing on the first launch.

48
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:53.000
To fix this, observe the stage mounted property on the RCT fabric surface.

49
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:59.000
Use it to trigger a native loading indicator while the JavaScript bundle finishes its evaluation.

50
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:06.000
Another frequent issue occurs when native inputs fail to push react native content upward to avoid the iOS keyboard.

51
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:14.000
React native manages its own keyboard behavior. You must explicitly implement keyboard avoiding view on the JavaScript side to handle the offset.

52
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:23.000
If the app crashes because a turbo module cannot be found, verify that your objective C class names perfectly match the turbo module registry.

53
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:34.000
By strictly respecting the C++ boundaries of these two runtimes, engineering teams can migrate legacy iOS applications screen by screen, maintaining native level responsiveness.

